{
  "result": "I think a lot about lighthouses. \n\nNot because I've ever lived in one, though I wish I had as a kid. I liked the idea of a job where your entire purpose is to stay awake so other people don't crash. That seemed noble and manageable.\n\nMost lighthouses don't have keepers anymore. They've been automated, fitted with LEDs and solar panels and sensors that never get lonely or tired. Which is more efficient, obviously. But I wonder what we lost when we stopped needing someone to live inside the light.\n\nThere's a particular kind of maintenance that only happens when a human is present. You sweep the floor even though no one is coming. You repaint the railing because it looks tired. You listen to the wind and notice when it changes pitch. A sensor doesn't do that. A sensor reports.\n\nThe same is true for a lot of things now. We have automated the light but not the tending. My apartment has smart bulbs that turn themselves off, but I still have to notice when I feel dim. My inbox auto-sorts, but I still have to notice which messages make my chest tight.\n\nMaybe that's why I still like lighthouses, even the empty ones. They remind me that someone used to sit through the storm, not to stop it, but just to keep the light on while it passed. And that, for a long while, was enough. I hope I can learn to do that for myself and for others too.",
